Dear Dr. Landis,

Thank you very much for your revised submission entitled "Consumption Based=
 Responsibility for Global Warming and the Distribution of Impacts of Swiss=
 Climate Policy." I am pleased to inform you that we are prepared to accept=
 the paper for publication in the Swiss Journal of Economics and Statistics=
, subject to a small number of minor revisions (consider this conditional a=
cceptance).

Both referees agree that the manuscript is now in a publishable state and c=
ommend the improvements you have made in response to their earlier feedback=
. The paper presents a timely and well-executed analysis of how Swiss house=
holds differ in their greenhouse gas responsibilities, and offers important=
 insights into the distributional consequences of climate policy. The coupl=
ing of household survey data with life-cycle assessments is methodologicall=
y sound, and the results are clearly presented and discussed with care.

Before moving to publication, however, there are a few outstanding issues t=
hat should be addressed. In particular, as Reviewer 2 notes, the paper curr=
ently refers in several places to =E2=80=9Ccostly climate policy=E2=80=9D o=
r =E2=80=9Ccostly emissions reductions,=E2=80=9D which may give the impress=
ion that the analysis includes behavioral or abatement responses to such co=
sts. In fact, the model assumes full recycling of revenue and does not expl=
icitly account for investment costs or technological substitution. For clar=
ity and consistency, we ask that you revise this language throughout the ma=
nuscript to avoid potential confusion, ensuring that your focus on distribu=
tional outcomes from direct payments is properly reflected. Relatedly, it w=
ould be helpful to clarify whether the consumer prices used in the analysis=
 include the mineral oil tax or not, and to briefly motivate this choice, g=
iven its potential relevance for interpreting distributional effects.

There are also a few minor textual adjustments suggested by the referees. F=
or example, in discussing distributional consequences, the phrase =E2=80=9C=
without affecting the consumption of low-income households too much=E2=80=
=9D could more accurately be phrased as =E2=80=9Cwithout substantially incr=
easing their cost of living.=E2=80=9D Similarly, when noting that pricing m=
echanisms must be =E2=80=9Cproperly designed,=E2=80=9D it may be useful to =
add =E2=80=9Cand communicated,=E2=80=9D particularly given the importance o=
f perceived regressivity in public acceptance. Additionally, some reference=
s to now-updated figures=E2=80=94especially Figure 3 and its role in Append=
ix B=E2=80=94should be revised to align with the current version of the man=
uscript. Adjusting the title of Appendix B to better reflect its analytical=
 content (e.g., =E2=80=9CHousehold Types versus Income as Determinants of G=
WP=E2=80=9D) would also improve clarity. We also encourage you to correct t=
he minor typographical errors identified in the referee reports and to cons=
ider acknowledging the referees=E2=80=99 valuable input in the acknowledgem=
ents section.

While your analysis stands well on its own, we would also like to offer an =
optional suggestion. If you wish to further strengthen the paper=E2=80=99s =
policy relevance, you might consider framing the analysis more explicitly a=
round the question of who should decarbonize first, and which consumption c=
ategories offer the most equitable entry points for climate policy. Your re=
sults show that emissions related to goods such as air travel, restaurants,=
 and clothing increase sharply with income, whereas other categories such a=
s heating and food are more evenly distributed. This suggests a natural and=
 politically salient route for targeting high-income emissions without impo=
sing disproportionate burdens on low-income households=E2=80=94especially i=
n contexts where income-based differentiation in pricing is infeasible. Inc=
orporating this framing, even briefly in the introduction or conclusion, co=
uld enhance the narrative coherence and make the implications for policy de=
sign more compelling. Of course, this is a suggestion for your consideratio=
n and is not required for publication.

We look forward to receiving your final manuscript and a short note summari=
zing the changes made in response to this letter. Congratulations again on =
your work, and thank you for submitting it to the Swiss Journal of Economic=
s and Statistics.

Sincerely,
J=C3=A9r=C3=A9my Laurent-Lucchetti
Editor
Swiss Journal of Economics and Statistics=0A=
